PART 2: ADVANCED CONCEPTS IN CLOUD COMPUTING
________________________________________
1. Intercloud & Cloud Federation
	Definition: An interconnected "cloud of clouds" enabling resource sharing, workload migration, and data exchange across distinct Cloud Service Providers.
	Classifications:
		- Federation of Clouds
		- Multicloud
	Topologies: Centralized Intercloud Federation : central cloud is the middleman for managing connections and resources.
				Peer-to-Peer Intercloud Federation : equal partners. negotiate and procure resources as needed.
	Benefits: Prevents vendor lock-in (not stuck with one single provider), expands geographic reach ( allowed to use datacentres from all countries)
			, enhances application fault-tolerance (if existing server crash, can switch to a back up),
			and optimizes energy consumption via cross-data-center VM migration.
	Challenges: Establishing trust mechanisms, unifying billing models, cross-provider SLA enforcement, and identity federation.
			Uses open standards like XMPP, OCCI, and CIMI3536.
________________________________________
2. Cloud Management & DMTF ( Distributed Management task force) Reference Architecture
	Core Management Functions: Resource provisioning
							   Service Level Management (SLA tracking), 
							   cost optimization (auto-scaling, spot instances),
							   task orchestration
	DMTF Reference Architecture Components:
	Cloud Service Developer: Designs and maintains service templates.
	Cloud Service Consumer: Accesses and utilizes cloud services.
	Cloud Service Provider: Supplies infrastructure and platform services.
	Provider Interface & Profiles: Standardized specifications defining management domain properties.
	Management Security: Centralized Cloud Management Platforms (CMPs) like AWS Console, OpenStack, and VMware vRealize represent high-value targets; 
						compromising the management portal risks the entire enterprise infrastructure.
________________________________________
3. Mobile Cloud Computing (MCC)
	Definition: An infrastructure where data storage and processing occur outside the mobile device, offloading heavy computations to cloud data centers or local cloud.
	Benefits: Extends smartphone battery life, 
			  overcomes local RAM/storage constraints,
			  improves app scalability 
			  data reliability
	Technical Challenges: High job offloading costs (bandwidth consumption), 
						  user mobility tracking across cellular networks, 
						  network latency,
						  live VM migration overheads
________________________________________
4. Media Cloud
	Definition: Specialized cloud architecture designed for storing, transcoding, rendering, and streaming large-scale multimedia content (HD/4K/VR video, audio)41more_horiz.
	5 Key Architectural Components:
	- Cloud Administrative Services: Billing, user role control, system monitoring.
	- Ingest Services: Receiving media inputs from cameras, broadcasts, or uploads.
	- Streaming Services: Real-time delivery, buffering, and protocol handling (TCP, UDP, RTP).
	- Video Services: Transcoding, format conversion, and multi-resolution.
	- Storage Subsystems: Multi-tier caching, long-term archival, and CDN integration.
________________________________________
5. Interoperability & Standards
	Interoperability: The capability to seamlessly move workloads, virtual machine disk images, and data across heterogeneous cloud platforms without vendor lock-in.
	Key Open Standards:
		OCCI (Open Cloud Computing Interface): Protocol specifications for IaaS management tasks.
		CIMI (Cloud Infrastructure Management Interface): Standardized interface for managing cloud infrastructure.
		Simple Cloud API: Unified interface for cloud storage and compute services.
	Impact: Standardized formats benefit IaaS most significantly, allowing VM images and block storage volumes to migrate freely across cloud vendors.
________________________________________
6. Cloud Governance
	Governance vs. Management: While Management addresses day-to-day operations and resource allocation, Governance sets high-level policies, strategic direction, risk management frameworks, and long-term oversight.
	Key Governance Responsibilities: SLA enforcement, data privacy compliance, resource usage auditing, access policy enforcement, and multitenancy risk mitigation.
	Governance Models: Lifecycle Governance Model and the Lewam Woldu Governance Mode.
________________________________________
7. Computational Intelligence (CI) in Cloud
	Role of CI: Employs Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning (ML), Neural Networks, and Genetic Algorithms to solve complex cloud optimization problems.
	NP-Hard Nature of Scheduling: Task and workflow scheduling in large-scale clouds are NP-hard problems (no exact solution in polynomial time); CI provides near-optimal approximate solutions efficiently.
	Applications: Predictive auto-scaling, 
				  real-time fault and anomaly detection, 
				  dynamic energy management,
				  automated security threat monitoring.
________________________________________
8. Green Cloud Computing
	Objective: Reducing electrical power consumption, carbon footprints, and operational costs (OPEX) in large data centers.
	Core Efficiency Metrics:
		- PowerUsageEffectiveness(PUE)=TotalFacilityEnergy/ITEquipmentEnergy
		- DataCenterInfrastructureEfficiency(DCiE)=1/PUE
	Hardware Power Management Techniques:
		Dynamic Voltage Scaling (DVS): Adjusts supply voltage to match real-time workload demands.
		Dynamic Frequency Scaling (DFS): Throttles CPU clock frequency to reduce power draw and heat.
		Dynamic Shutdown (DNS): Powers down completely idle servers and network switches.
	Virtualization & Server Consolidation: Live migrating virtual machines onto fewer physical nodes so idle servers can be safely shut down.
	Architectural Frameworks:
		GreenCloud Simulator: Evaluates energy consumption across compute and communication elements (where over 30% of energy is spent).
		GreenBroker (Garg & Buyya): An intermediate cloud broker that tracks provider carbon emissions to schedule jobs on the most eco-friendly infrastructure.
